A local library maintains a list of book ID numbers (unique integers) that are not initially sorted. The librarian wants the list to be arranged in ascending order. (4)

Write a C program that:

1. Takes as input a list of book IDs (unsorted).
2. Sorts the IDs using Bubble Sort.
3. Displays the sorted list.

Marking Scheme:
- Variable declaration, reading limit and array elements: 1 M
- Logic for bubble sort: 1M
- Result display for sorting and binary seacrh: 1M

```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
int n, i, j, temp, key;
int low, high, mid, result = -1;
printf("Enter number of Book IDs: ");
scanf("%d", &n);
int arr[n];
printf("Enter %d Book IDs: ", n);
for(i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    scanf("%d", &arr[i]);
}
// Bubble Sort
for(i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
    for(j = 0; j < n-i-1; j++) {
        if(arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
            temp = arr[j];
            arr[j] = arr[j+1];
            arr[j+1] = temp;
        }
    }
}
printf("Sorted Book IDs: ");
for(i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    printf("%d ", arr[i]);
}
printf("\n");
return 0;
}
```

Write a C program to compute the summation for the following series using `while` loops up to `n` terms. The variable `n` is a user input. (3)

$$
1 + 3\left(\frac{1}{9}\right) + 12\left(\frac{1}{9}\right)^2 + 48\left(\frac{1}{9}\right)^3 + \cdots
$$

```c
#include<stdio.h>
int main() {
float sum=1.0;
int n;
int m=3;
float term=1/9.0;
int t_count =1;
printf("Enter the number of terms you want to add in the series\n");
scanf("%d",&n); (½ Marks)

/*Using while loop*/ (2 Marks)
while(t_count< n)
{
sum += m * term;
m*=4;
term = term *(1/9.0);
t_count++;
}
printf("The sum is:%f", sum); (½ Marks)
}
```

